/* book: lead: A voltage source inverter holds its DC voltage steady with a capacitor and lets the load decide the current. A current source inverter (CSI) does the opposite: a large series inductor Ld holds the DC link current Id almost constant, and the bridge steers that current into the load lines. The output current is a rectangular wave of height Id and the load voltage is whatever the load needs. CSIs sur */
