/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A CSI is fed from a controlled rectifier or a chopper through Ld.
   - Because the load voltage reverses every half cycle, an off device sees reverse voltage.
   - Four thyristors T1 to T4 form a bridge fed with Id, and a capacitor C is connected across the load.
   - Commutation is done by the capacitor.
   - tc is the circuit turn-off time in seconds; it must exceed the thyristor turn-off time tq.
   - The three-phase form of this circuit, with commutating capacitors and series diodes that isolate them from the load, is the auto-sequential commutated inverter (ASCI).
   - Six devices conduct for 120° each, two at a time, one from the upper group and one from the lower.
   - I1 = (√6/π)Id = 0.780Id, Irms = 0.816Id I1 is the rms fundamental line current and Irms the rms of the whole wave.
   - The 120° shape has no triplen harmonics (cos 3 × 30° = 0); the 5th is 20 % of the fundamental, the 7th 14.3 %, the 11th 9.1 % and the 13th 7.7 %, a THD of 31.1 %.
   - A load-commutated inverter (LCI) uses the back EMF of an over-excited synchronous motor, which runs at a leading power factor, to turn its thyristors off, and drives motors of several megawatts in pumps, fans and compressors.
*/
