/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A two-level leg gives a pole voltage of +Vdc/2 or −Vdc/2.
   - ΔV = Vdc/(m − 1), line levels = 2m − 1 ΔV is the step in volts, Vdc the total bus voltage and m the number of pole-voltage levels.
   - Each switch in a clamped leg blocks only ΔV, so on a 3.3 kV bus each switch of a three-level leg blocks only 1.65 kV.
   - Diode-clamped (neutral-point-clamped, NPC).
   - Flying capacitor.
   - Level-shifted carrier PWM compares one sine with m − 1 stacked triangles; phase-shifted carriers are used for flying-capacitor and CHB cells so each cell switches equally.
   - More levels cost more devices, gate drivers, capacitors and sensors.
*/
