/* book Worked example 2: Q: A cascaded H-bridge phase has 3 cells of 100 V. It makes a staircase with switching angles α1 = 11.5°, α2 = 28.72°, α3 = 57.11° (cell k switches to +100 V at αk). Find the number of levels, the peak voltage, b1, and show that the 5th and 7th are eliminated.
   1. Levels m = 2s + 1 = 7; peak = sVcell = 300 V.
   2. Each cell gives a pulse of width 180° − 2αk, so bn = (4Vcell/nπ)(cos nα1 + cos nα2 + cos nα3), with 4Vcell/π = 127.32 V.
   3. n = 1: cos 11.5° + cos 28.72° + cos 57.11° = 0.9799 + 0.8770 + 0.5430 = 2.3999, so b1 = 305.6 V peak, 80 % of the 382.0 V of three square waves.
   4. n = 5: cos 57.5° + cos 143.6° + cos 285.55° = 0.5373 + -0.8049 + 0.2681 = 0.0005 ≈ 0.
   5. n = 7: cos 80.5° + cos 201.04° + cos 399.77° = 0.1650 + -0.9333 + 0.7686 = 0.0003 ≈ 0.
   6. The 3rd is not zero (b3 = 4.1 V), but it cancels in the line voltage of a three-phase star; the next line harmonics are the 11th and 13th, at 1.1 V and 10.1 V.
   Answer: 7 levels, 300 V peak, b1 = 305.6 V; b5 and b7 are zero; the 11th and 13th are 1.1 V and 10.1 V. */
