/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The grid is a stiff voltage source: its amplitude and frequency barely move when one inverter connects.
   - i* is the current reference in amperes, I* its peak, θ the grid angle from the PLL and φ* the commanded phase shift (0 for unity power factor).
   - Single-phase power is P = V I cos φ and Q = V I sin φ, with V and I rms values and φ the angle between them; in three-phase, P = √3 VL I cos φ with VL the line voltage.
   - The current loop must be much faster than the DC-link loop: a link loop of 10 to 20 Hz bandwidth, a current loop of 1 to 2 kHz bandwidth, with the bridge switching and sampling at 10 to 20 kHz.
   - Hysteresis control switches the bridge whenever the current leaves a band of width h around i*.
   - fres = (1/2π)√((L1 + L2)/(L1 L2 C)) L1 is the inverter-side inductance, L2 the grid-side inductance and C the filter capacitance; fres in hertz.
   - An LCL filter attenuates the switching ripple at 60 dB per decade, far better than a single L, with less inductance.
   - If the grid is disconnected for repair, an inverter that kept feeding local loads would energise the line and endanger the line crew.
   - An off-grid (stand-alone) inverter has no grid to follow, so it controls its own voltage amplitude and frequency, often with droop so that several units share the load.
*/
