/* book exam traps: A grid-tied inverter commands current, not voltage; its voltage is whatever the grid plus the filter drop needs. | The sine table uses the peak I*, cable and meter ratings use the rms; do not mix them. | In three-phase use P = √3 VL I cos φ with the line voltage; using the phase voltage gives a current √3 times too large. | An LCL filter is not just a big L: undamped, its resonance can make the current loop oscillate. | Anti-islanding means the inverter must stop when the grid is lost; an off-grid inverter is designed to run alone. */
